course: religion-ethics
lesson: 91
title: Owning up when it is hard
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some owning up is hard because it costs you: in front of the class, at a friend's house, or when someone else already took the blame for you.
explain: Picture a football flying through old Mrs Kowalski's greenhouse; all the kids leg it (run away), but Mia stops and goes back, which is brave and honest; most neighbours forgive a child who comes back and owns up.
explain: The ancient Greek thinker Aristotle said courage is doing the right thing in spite of fear, and the Bible says, "Do not follow the crowd in doing wrong" (Exodus 23:2).
explain: The hardest case is when someone innocent is punished for your fault; letting it go on is "false witness by silence", so you speak up even if it means trouble for you.
explain: Owning up fast matters most when someone could be hurt, like a missing guinea pig: then it is about safety as well as honesty.
fact: Online, things last: deleting a mean post is not owning up; saying sorry to the person directly works far better (Luke 8:17: "There is nothing hidden that will not be disclosed").
fact: Being a guest means caring for other people's things as if they were your own; "Give to everyone what you owe them" (Romans 13:7).
fact: Letting someone believe something false on purpose, like Dad being cross with "some idiot" in a car park, is close to lying.
fact: The commandment against false witness protects even strangers' names (Exodus 20:16).
fact: "The righteous care for the needs of their animals" (Proverbs 12:10); when an animal could be hurt, tell straight away so everyone can search.
fact: "Each one should carry their own load" (Galatians 6:5); people trust a leader who says "my fault".
fact: "Do not spread false reports" (Exodus 23:1); blaming a brother for what he didn't do wears away trust at home.
fact: If a lost thing you were paid for turns up, take it back and tell them: "We are taking pains to do what is right, not only in the eyes of the Lord but also in the eyes of man" (2 Corinthians 8:21).
fact: God sees what is hidden (Psalm 139:1-4); honesty is the same at home, at school and at church.
fact: "Have nothing to do with a false charge and do not put an innocent or honest person to death" (Exodus 23:7): letting someone be punished for you is false witness by silence.
fact: Almost every tradition agrees that punishing the innocent is a serious injustice.
fact: Aristotle said courage is doing the right thing in spite of fear.
fact: Slang check: "leg it" means run away; "our kid" (Manchester, Liverpool) means my brother or sister; "chuck it" means throw it away.
exercise: Ben posted a mean joke and deleted it, but someone screenshotted it. What should he do? | Say sorry to the classmate directly and in the chat | Say his phone was hacked by someone else | Delete his whole account and start again | Do nothing, because the post is gone
exercise: Why is deleting a post not the same as owning up? | Things online last, and the hurt still happened | Deleting always removes it from every phone that saw it | Because only teachers can delete posts | Because a deleted post never happened
exercise: Aisha broke a mug at Hannah's. "Just chuck it in the bin, she won't notice," says Hannah. What should Aisha do? | Tell Hannah's mum, say sorry, and tell her own parents too | Chuck it in the bin like Hannah said, so nobody gets upset | Hide the pieces in her coat pocket | Blame Hannah's little brother
exercise: Leo's scooter scraped Dad's car. Dad blames "some idiot" at the supermarket. What should Leo do? | Tell Dad, because he's cross with a stranger who did nothing | Say nothing, since the stranger will never know he was blamed | Agree that it was some idiot at the supermarket | Fix the scratch secretly with a felt pen
exercise: Owain left the guinea pig's cage open, and it's missing. Why must he tell straight away? | So everyone can start searching before it gets hurt | So he gets the punishment over with quickly | Because guinea pigs can tell who left the door open | Because the teacher will check the cameras
exercise: Sophie forgot the poster and her group did badly. The teacher asks what happened. What is best? | That was me, I forgot it, sorry everyone | Our group just wasn't very good at posters this time | Someone must have taken our poster from the classroom | The teacher didn't explain what the poster was for
exercise: Kyle says, "Wasn't me, our kid did it," but his brother wasn't home. What does "our kid" mean? | My brother or sister | My own son or daughter | My best friend from school | The family dog or cat
exercise: Molly's family paid for a lost library book. A month later she finds it. What should she do? | Take it back and tell them; let the library decide | Keep it, because they already paid and it belongs to them now | Sell it, because it now belongs to her | Throw it away so nobody asks
exercise: The ball smashes Mrs Kowalski's greenhouse, and all the kids leg it. What does "leg it" mean? | Run away | Kick the ball | Stand still | Fall over
exercise: Why is Mia brave to go back to the greenhouse? | She does the right thing even though she's scared and alone | Because she wanted to get the ball back | Because the other kids told her to | Because she knew she wouldn't be blamed
exercise: Theo spilled paint on the church carpet and hid it under a chair. What should he do? | Tell the vicar; it's only paint | Leave it, God won't notice in church | Move the chair every week | Blame the younger children
exercise: Jamal's little brother was punished for something Jamal did. What should Jamal do? | Speak up now, even if it means trouble for him | Keep quiet, because it's over now and can't be changed | Give his brother some sweets instead | Wait until his brother forgets
exercise: Why is letting your brother be punished for you so serious? | Punishing the innocent is a serious injustice | Because brothers always find out the truth in the end | Because parents can read minds and will soon know | It isn't serious if he's younger and soon forgets
exercise: What did Aristotle say courage is? | Doing the right thing in spite of fear | Never feeling afraid of anything at all, ever | Running away from danger as fast as you can | Winning fights against kids who are bigger than you
exercise: What does Exodus 23:2 say? | Do not follow the crowd in doing wrong | Always stay with your friends, whatever they do | Run when everyone else runs, so you're not left alone | Never go into old people's gardens without asking
test: Your mate says, "Just delete it, then it never happened." What is the best reply? | Screenshots last; say sorry to the person instead | Delete it twice to be extra sure it's gone from everyone's phone | Post another one to cover it up | Blame autocorrect for the joke
test: Hannah says, "Mum's in the garden, she'll never know about the mug, sorted." What would be better? | Tell her mum, because a guest cares for others' things | Hide the mug at the back of the cupboard | Glue it and put it back on the shelf without saying a word | Leave before her mum comes back in
test: Dad is fuming: "Some idiot scraped my car in the car park!" Leo stays quiet. Is staying quiet OK? | No; letting Dad believe something false on purpose is close to lying | Yes; Dad said it, not Leo, so it's not a lie | Yes; the stranger will never find out | No, but only because Dad might check the cameras
test: Owain thinks, "If I wait, maybe the guinea pig comes back and nobody knows." What would happen if he waits? | The search starts late, and the guinea pig could be hurt or lost | The guinea pig will come back faster | Nothing; guinea pigs always come home | Someone else will be blamed, which is fine
test: "Who left the tap running?" Kyle: "Our kid did it." His brother's at football. Is it "just a joke"? | No; blaming him for what he didn't do wears away trust | Yes; brothers always blame each other, so it's fine and nobody minds | Yes, because taps aren't important | No, but only because Dad found out
test: A cartoon shows kids who run from a broken greenhouse and the one who goes back gets laughed at. What would really often happen? | Most neighbours forgive children who come back and own up | The child who goes back always goes to prison | The neighbour gives the runaways a prize | Nobody would ever find out who did it, so going back is pointless
test: Theo says, "It's church, God's busy, he won't care about a stain." What does the vicar say? | God sees what is hidden; I'd rather have a stained carpet than a hidden fault | God only looks at churches on Sundays | Stains in church are the vicar's problem | Theo should buy a bigger chair
test: Molly's mum says, "We paid for it, it's ours now." Molly finds the book. What is the fairest step? | Take it back and tell them; the library may refund or just be glad | Keep it, because paying makes it yours | Lend it to a friend for a month | Hide it so nobody has to decide
test: Jamal thinks, "If I tell now, I'll be in loads of trouble." Why should he still tell? | His brother deserves justice and his parents deserve the truth | Because he'll get a reward for telling | Because his brother will find out anyway and tell their parents | He shouldn't; it's too late now
test: Sophie's friend says, "Just blame the printer for the poster." What is the problem? | It's a lie, and owning up protects your team's good name | Printers can't be blamed for anything | The teacher already knows about printers | There's no problem; printers break all the time, so everyone believes it
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the permitting of an innocent person to suffer punishment for one's own fault? | A grave injustice, constituting false witness by silence | A matter of indifference provided that the innocent person is younger | A commendable instance of discretion | A form of loyalty to one's family
exam: Given that a guest damages an article belonging to the host, identify the course of action most consistent with this lesson. | Disclosure to the host, together with an apology and notification of one's own parents | Concealment of the damaged article in a receptacle for refuse | Attribution of the damage to another member of the household | Departure from the premises prior to the host's return
exam: Select the reason for which the prompt disclosure of an open cage is of particular importance. | The welfare of an animal may depend upon an immediate search | The animal is capable of identifying the culpable person upon its return | The teacher is obliged to consult surveillance recordings | The cage must be repaired before the end of the day
exam: Ascertain which statement concerning courage is consistent with the view attributed to Aristotle. | Courage consists in doing what is right notwithstanding fear | Courage consists in the complete absence of fear | Courage consists in the defeat of all one's rivals in combat | Courage consists in the avoidance of all danger
